---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-b5`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin B5 (Pantothenic Acid) Reference',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamins & Minerals', item: `${SITE}/vitamins` },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Vitamin B5', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin B5 (Pantothenic Acid): CoA, Acne Claims, and the Panto Nail Story"
  description="Pantothenic acid — the vitamin behind coenzyme A. What the evidence shows for the megadose-acne claim, panthenol in cosmetics, and why deficiency is essentially unheard of."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamins", label: "Vitamins & Minerals" }, { href: "/vitamin-b5", label: "Vitamin B5" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin B5 (Pantothenic Acid)</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      The name comes from Greek <em>pantothen</em> — "from everywhere" — reflecting how
      widespread it is in food. Pantothenic acid is the backbone of coenzyme A, essential
      for fatty acid synthesis, oxidation, and the citric acid cycle. Isolated deficiency
      is essentially unknown in normal diets.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Daily Adequate Intake</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li><strong>Adults:</strong> 5 mg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> 6 mg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Lactation:</strong> 7 mg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>No Tolerable Upper Intake Level</strong> — very high doses (10 g) can cause diarrhoea and GI upset.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The acne megadose claim</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      A single small 2014 double-blind RCT reported pantothenic acid 2,200 mg/day for 12
      weeks reduced total facial lesion count vs placebo in mild-to-moderate acne. The
      claim went viral in supplement marketing, but the trial was small (n = 48), the
      mechanism is speculative, and no larger replication has been published. It's a
      possible-but-unproven adjunct at best.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Panthenol (provitamin B5)</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      D-panthenol is the alcohol form of pantothenic acid used in shampoos, skin creams,
      and eye drops. It penetrates skin and hair, humectant properties help retain moisture,
      and there's reasonable evidence for wound-healing acceleration and modest improvement
      in hair shaft appearance. Topical use is well tolerated.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Deficiency</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Experimentally induced deficiency causes "burning feet syndrome" (severe paraesthesia
      and burning pain in feet and lower legs), fatigue, headaches, and insomnia. Real-world
      cases are limited to severe undernutrition and one wartime study of malnourished POWs
      whose symptoms responded to pantothenate.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Food sources</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li>Beef liver (3 oz): 8.3 mg — one serving covers 165% of the AI</li>
      <li>Shiitake mushrooms (½ cup cooked): 2.6 mg</li>
      <li>Sunflower seeds (¼ cup): 2.4 mg</li>
      <li>Chicken breast (3 oz): 1.3 mg</li>
      <li>Tuna (3 oz): 1.2 mg</li>
      <li>Avocado (½ fruit): 1.0 mg</li>
      <li>Egg (1 large): 0.7 mg</li>
      <li>Milk (1 cup): 0.9 mg</li>
      <li>Broccoli (½ cup): 0.4 mg</li>
    </ul>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> There is little reason for isolated B5
      supplementation — it comes bundled in every B-complex and is abundant in food.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-b5" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
